[7.85, "o", "Claude\r\n"]
[8.15, "o", "  Here are the top three on the private\r\n  leaderboard, summarized from each team's\r\n  Kaggle writeup:\r\n"]
[8.27, "o", "\r\n  1. NVARC (ivan, CPMP) — 24.03%\r\n  • Most of their gain came from synthetic\r\n"]
[8.39, "o", "    data. gpt-oss-120b read human descriptions\r\n    of puzzles (from H-ARC and BARC), merged\r\n    pairs of them into about 266k new, harder\r\n"]
[8.51, "o", "    puzzle descriptions, then wrote Python\r\n    generators for the inputs and outputs.\r\n    They kept about 103k puzzles whose output\r\n"]
[8.63, "o", "    programs agreed with each other.\r\n  • They trained a Qwen3 model (4B, plus a 2B\r\n    variant) on about 3.2M augmented samples\r\n"]
[8.75, "o", "    using the 2024 winning method from the\r\n    ARChitects. For each test task they fine-\r\n    tuned a LoRA adapter, then searched for\r\n"]
[13.37, "o", "    answers with a batched depth-first search\r\n    and re-scored the candidates under\r\n    augmentations.\r\n"]
[13.49, "o", "  • In the final days they added a Tiny\r\n    Recursive Model (TRM) to the ensemble, but\r\n    had little time to tune it.\r\n"]
[13.61, "o", "\r\n  2. The ARChitects (Franzen, Disselhoff,\r\n  Hartmann) — 16.53%\r\n"]
[13.73, "o", "  • They won in 2024 with a model that writes\r\n    the answer one token at a time (Mistral-\r\n    NeMo-Minitron-8B). For the first half of\r\n"]
[13.85, "o", "    2025 they improved it with per-task fine-\r\n    tuning, faster speculative-decoding search\r\n    and more scoring augmentations, which\r\n"]
[13.97, "o", "    peaked at about 17% public.\r\n  • They then switched to a masked-diffusion\r\n    LLM (LLaDA-8B) that produces the whole\r\n"]
